window.RC_PLAYBACK = {"Title": {"video": "videos/Title.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Hi everyone. This is optical PPO: Pasha's scheme for training a Kerr-ring reservoir with reinforcement learning, built on work by Pasha, Lida, Shi-Yuan, Mahmoud and Mohammad.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.7333, "end": 11.0333, "kind": "loop", "note": "Underneath is the real thing: a cart-pole played by the trained ring. The state goes in as tones of light, a ring answers with a comb, and the comb picks the next push.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Intro": {"video": "videos/Intro.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "What we did, in one sentence: we taught the light in a Kerr microring to play simple control games, with reinforcement learning. Let me first say what each of those words means.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "IntroWhat": {"video": "videos/IntroWhat.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.9333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Let me set the scene from the start. Reinforcement learning: an agent acts, the world answers with a reward, and from that alone it has to work out what to do in each situation.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 0.9333, "end": 1.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Usually the agent is a neural network and every weight is trained. Reservoir computing is the lazy version: a fixed physical system turns the input into rich features, and we train only a linear readout on top of it.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.8333, "end": 2.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Our physical system is a Kerr microring. A few laser tones go in. The Kerr effect mixes them, and out comes a frequency comb with many more lines than went in.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.7333, "end": 3.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Optical PPO, Pasha's scheme, puts the three together. The ring plus one small matrix is the agent, and PPO, a standard reinforcement-learning algorithm, trains that matrix. The ring itself is never trained.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.6333, "end": 4.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "So the question for today: how far do one fixed ring and a few dozen trained numbers get on control games? Here are the games.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Games": {"video": "videos/Games.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 6.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Three standard control games. CartPole: push the cart left or right so the pole stays up. What you see is the trained ring playing.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 6.3333, "end": 14.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pendulum: the motor is too weak to lift it, so it has to pump the swing first, then catch it at the top.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 14.4333, "end": 20.6167, "kind": "normal", "note": "LunarLander: three engines, land between the flags. On Monday Shi-Yuan wondered whether this one needs a nonlinearity at all. We'll come back to that.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "SystemLoop": {"video": "videos/SystemLoop.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Here is everything at a glance. Reinforcement learning is a loop: a game hands over its state, an agent answers with an action, again and again. For CartPole the state is four numbers.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.2333, "end": 4.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Our agent is made of light. Each number is written onto its own laser tone, next to a strong pump.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.6333, "end": 7.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The tones go into a Kerr microring. The ring mixes them and answers with a frequency comb: seventeen lines, and we measure the power of each.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.4333, "end": 11.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Those seventeen powers go through one matrix, the readout. It picks the action, and the game moves on. That matrix is the only thing reinforcement learning trains; the ring is never touched.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 11.0333, "end": 17.3333, "kind": "loop", "note": "Round it goes, fifty times a second. Every bar and line here is from a real game.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Overview": {"video": "videos/Overview.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Mohammad asked for this as a reservoir-computing problem, with the ring as the reservoir. So, six parts. One: what goes in and what comes out.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 0.8333, "end": 1.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Two: the encoding, how a number becomes light.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.6333, "end": 2.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Three: the reservoir itself, a Kerr microring, and what it does to the light.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.4333, "end": 3.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Four: training the readout with PPO. This is where most of the time goes: how the weights actually get updated.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.2333, "end": 4.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Five: why the reservoir is useful at all.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.0333, "end": 4.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Six: what comes next.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "RcProblems": {"video": "videos/RcProblems.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Before part one, why bother. From what I've seen, optical reservoir computing in the literature has three recurring problems. Sometimes, not always: it is beaten by a plain fully connected linear network. It is very hard to build. And it claims high energy efficiency for a setup that almost certainly is not efficient. Can we do better?", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.3333, "end": 3.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "On the first two: yes, I think we can. In part five, Pendulum is a task where the ring beats a linear readout. And the scheme asks the lab only for tones in and line powers out.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.7333, "end": 5.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "On energy: probably not. But that's okay; efficiency is not the claim here.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Sec1": {"video": "videos/Sec1.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "First, what the game gives us and what we give back: a few numbers in, one of a few actions out.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.1333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "StateNumbers": {"video": "videos/StateNumbers.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 7.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "CartPole's whole state is four numbers: cart position and speed, pole angle and how fast it turns. Each bar is in units of that number's typical size.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.3333, "end": 15.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pendulum has three: the angle as its cosine and its sine, plus how fast it turns. Here the trained ring swings it up from hanging.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 15.5333, "end": 22.2163, "kind": "normal", "note": "The lander has eight: position, velocity, tilt, how fast it tilts, and two switches for the legs touching down. No pixels, just a handful of numbers.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Moves": {"video": "videos/Moves.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The other end: every step the game wants exactly one action back, out of a fixed handful. Two for CartPole, three for Pendulum, four for the lander.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.2333, "end": 8.1833, "kind": "normal", "note": "All three at once, scripted. CartPole: push left, push right. Pendulum: full torque one way, none, the other way. The lander: nothing, or one of three engines; the arrows show which way each pushes it. The agent gives every action a weight and draws one from them: that is part four.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "BoxDimensions": {"video": "videos/BoxDimensions.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 6.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Between the input and the output sits the feature space. Each game's numbers go in as tones: four, three, eight. The same ring answers with a comb, and we read seventeen of its lines, thirty-three for the lander: those powers are the features. One matrix takes them to the actions. Two times seventeen weights plus two biases is thirty-six numbers; fifty-four and a hundred and thirty-six for the other two. The red part is all we train.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "Sec2": {"video": "videos/Sec2.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Part two, the encoding: how a handful of numbers becomes light. Squash each number, write it onto its own tone, and choose how strong to make the tones.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.1333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Squash": {"video": "videos/Squash.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 3.9333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Back to the cart-pole's four numbers. Nothing stops a number from getting big, so before it touches the light it goes through this curve, a hyperbolic tangent.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.9333, "end": 10.2833, "kind": "normal", "note": "Follow one number: along to its value, up to the curve, across. Small values pass almost unchanged; big ones are held inside plus or minus one. Pendulum uses a hard clip.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 10.2833, "end": 15.5833, "kind": "loop", "note": "Live: the outlines are the raw numbers, the filled bars are what we pass on. They never leave the band.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "OneTonePerInput": {"video": "videos/OneTonePerInput.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 3.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Now the light. A frequency axis with numbered comb lines. Line zero carries the pump, always on. Lines one to four get one number each.", "auto": false, "after": 0.7}, {"start": 3.6333, "end": 11.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "A tone carrying zero sits at a resting height, epsilon. Plus one doubles it, minus one switches it off. The pump is about three times a resting tone.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 11.2333, "end": 16.5333, "kind": "loop", "note": "Live: four numbers, four tones, moving together. This is what goes into the ring, fifty times a second.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "ToneStrength": {"video": "videos/ToneStrength.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 7.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "And what is epsilon? Just a scale factor: how strong the tones are next to the pump. Watch it go from one, where we run, down by a factor of fifty and back. Nothing in the game fixes it. It is a hyperparameter, handed to us.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.3333, "end": 10.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The right value depends on what the ring is doing, its regime; the next slide shows those. Pasha's code has four settings of the ring, and their epsilons differ by a factor of fifty. Both his sweeps and mine show a best value for each. Tuning it on a real device we leave to the experimentalists.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "RegimeMap": {"video": "videos/RegimeMap.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 3.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "So what are these regimes? The tones go into a ring that is already pumped, and what that ring does on its own depends on two settings: how far the pump sits from the resonance, the detuning, and how hard it pumps. Here is that map, recomputed with Pasha's solver: every cell is one simulation started from noise, sorted by how uneven the light is around the ring and how much it flickers. Grey is no pattern: the light stays flat. Blue is Turing rolls, red is chaos, green is where a soliton survives if you seed one. The shaded wedge is where the ring has two flat states.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.5333, "end": 5.9333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The small pictures are the light around the ring at four points; three are presets in Pasha's code: the chaotic comb, Turing rolls and one soliton, three of the four settings on the last slide. The fourth, and the one every run in this talk uses, is a ring with the opposite dispersion, where the light stays flat: no pattern, whatever the tones do.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "BestEps": {"video": "videos/BestEps.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "This is where my own sweep comes in. For each regime I scanned the tone strength and asked how well a linear readout on the comb recovers the inputs, their pair products and their triple products. The inputs come through almost anywhere. The products do not: every regime has a window and a best epsilon. Push past it and the soliton and the rolls lose their pattern; stay below it and the chaotic comb hides the tones in its own noise. The triangles are the values in Pasha's code. So epsilon is a real knob, and its best value depends on the regime.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "RegimeSettles": {"video": "videos/RegimeSettles.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "One thing I played with. Pasha's code can run the ring in four regimes. In the one every run here uses, I switched the input from one state of a game to another and watched the seventeen lines: they relax to a new steady comb in about eight decay times and stay there. A control step lasts about a million of those, so in this regime one input gives one comb.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 5.4333, "end": 11.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The other three, same switch. The chaotic comb never settles, so its lines are read as a time average. Turing rolls creep. One soliton settles, but only on much weaker tones. This says how each regime has to be read, not how well it computes: the chaotic comb is strongly nonlinear, and each regime has its own best epsilon. Every run in this talk uses the no-pattern ring, Pasha's default.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Sec3": {"video": "videos/Sec3.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Part three, the reservoir: a Kerr microring. I won't go through how it works inside, only far enough to see where products of the inputs come from.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.1333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "KerrOnOff": {"video": "videos/KerrOnOff.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 7.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The reservoir is a microring next to a waveguide. Five lines of light go in: the pump and one tone per number. A comb comes out, and we read seventeen lines: twelve of them we never put in.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.2333, "end": 18.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Why new lines? The Kerr effect. The glass's index depends on the intensity, so the phase the light picks up in the ring depends on how much power is in it. In the equation for a line's amplitude a, that is a frequency omega plus g times a squared. Weak light only shifts the resonance; enough of it and the lines mix. The knob is g. At zero the ring is a plain filter and only our five lines come out. Turn it up and new lines grow. Every frame is a real solution at that strength.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 18.2333, "end": 24.9333, "kind": "normal", "note": "And the new lines depend on the input. I tilt the pole by hand: only tone three changes going in, but the whole comb answers.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "FourWaveMixing": {"video": "videos/FourWaveMixing.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 6.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Mohammad's equation for one line n has a loss, our drive, and the Kerr cross terms: g times a product of three line amplitudes, one of them conjugated. That product lands on line m2 plus m3 minus m1. The simplest case: the pump and two tones, on lines one and three. The product a zero star, a one, a three lands on line four, which nothing drives. Turn the Kerr up and line four lights.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 6.6333, "end": 13.4833, "kind": "normal", "note": "The pump is strong and hardly changes, so line four goes as a one times a three: the product of two inputs. Here is the solver's power on line four for every pair of the two tone amplitudes. Lower either one and it falls; with either tone off it is dark.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 13.4833, "end": 19.8167, "kind": "normal", "note": "Switch the middle tone back on: a Pendulum state, three tones. Every pair of tones, times the pump, lands on line i plus j, and pairs can share a line: a one a three and a two a two both land on line four. The lines further out come from products of these products. So each new line carries a sum of products of the inputs, and that is what the readout gets.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Detection": {"video": "videos/Detection.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 3.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Reading the ring: a photodiode behind each line gives its power. One frame of the comb is seventeen numbers, the feature vector phi. Red is above a line's usual value, blue below. From here on, phi is all the readout ever sees.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.8333, "end": 10.1333, "kind": "loop", "note": "Live, phi flickers with the game. Every detected value carries one percent of noise.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Sec4": {"video": "videos/Sec4.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Part four, the centre: how the one trained piece, the readout, learns. The method is PPO: first what that is, then exactly what it does to the weights.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.1333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WhatIsPPO": {"video": "videos/WhatIsPPO.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Reinforcement-learning methods come in two families. Value-based ones, like Q-learning, learn how good each action is and then take the best one. Policy-gradient methods learn the policy itself, the probabilities, and push them towards actions that paid off.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.5333, "end": 2.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "PPO, proximal policy optimization, Schulman 2017, is one of the second family. It is the one most used today: OpenAI's Dota bots were trained with it, and so was the human-feedback stage of InstructGPT, the model before ChatGPT.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.4333, "end": 3.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "What it does, plainly: it plays a batch of games, learns from that batch 20 times over, and clips every change so the policy cannot drift far from the one that played those games. The next slides go through that step by step.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WhyPolicyGradient": {"video": "videos/WhyPolicyGradient.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "A toy, to show why. One state, two actions: left pays off seventy percent of the time, right fifty. Two learners, each averaged over 2000 tries of the whole thing. The greedy one tries each action once, then always takes whichever has paid better so far. The policy-gradient one keeps a probability for each and nudges it after every try.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.5333, "end": 5.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Greedy commits to whatever looked best early. One unlucky first try and it never comes back: 19 percent of its runs never try left again. That is a bit like overfitting to the first games. The policy gradient keeps trying both, so it finds out, and its probabilities move smoothly. It also works with no change when the actions are continuous, and for us the policy is the readout, so the gradient lands right on W.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WhatIsRL": {"video": "videos/WhatIsRL.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 3.4, "kind": "normal", "note": "Now what that policy is, in one picture. The agent sees the state and holds a probability for each action: these two bars, which add up to one. That is the policy, pi of a given s. It draws its action from them and gets a point per step the pole stays up. This one knows nothing: down in thirteen steps. Slowed six times.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.4, "end": 10.933, "kind": "normal", "note": "A game's total reward is its return: the one thing the agent tries to raise. Ten updates in, it lasts three hundred steps before the cart runs off the track.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 10.933, "end": 17.333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The trained agent runs to the cap of five hundred. Twice real time.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "ReadoutIsPolicy": {"video": "videos/ReadoutIsPolicy.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Here is the policy for one state. The ring hands us seventeen line powers for it: call that vector phi of s, one number under each comb line.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.5333, "end": 4.9333, "kind": "normal", "note": "W is not the policy: it is the readout's weights, one column per line and one row per action. Red is a positive weight, blue a negative one. These are the trained values. Next to it, one bias per action: two times seventeen plus two, the thirty-six trained numbers. Here the biases came out close to zero.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.9333, "end": 8.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "A row's score: multiply each weight by the number above it, add up, and add that row's bias. That sum is the logit for that action.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 8.7333, "end": 10.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "A softmax turns the two logits into two probabilities. For this one state, those two numbers are the policy. We draw the action from them and the cart is pushed.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 10.7333, "end": 15.4333, "kind": "loop", "note": "Running live: phi and the two probabilities change every step, W does not. The same thirty-six numbers give the policy in every state, so we never need a table over all the states.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Rollout": {"video": "videos/Rollout.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "How PPO works, in five steps. Step one: play. Sixty-four games side by side, each with its own copy of the ring. This is the real batch of the fourth training iteration.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.5333, "end": 5.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Every step of every game is recorded: one row per game, one column per step. Slowed five times. A bright cell is a pole falling; that game starts over.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 5.0333, "end": 7.6667, "kind": "normal", "note": "Each recorded step holds four things: the state, the spectrum the ring answered with, the action, and the reward. The spectrum is stored here and never measured again.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Advantage": {"video": "videos/Advantage.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.6, "kind": "normal", "note": "Step two: score every recorded step. Take one game out of the batch. Replayed here: the pole falls partway along and a new game starts.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.6, "end": 7.1, "kind": "normal", "note": "A small network, the critic, guesses from each state how much reward is still to come: the curve V. G is what actually came. Before the fall, less came than expected.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.1, "end": 8.7667, "kind": "normal", "note": "The difference is the advantage. Red: better than expected. Blue: worse. That one number per step is the only signal the readout gets.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 8.7667, "end": 10.6667, "kind": "normal", "note": "Do it for all 2048 steps; PPO then centres and scales them across the batch. This coloured batch is what the readout learns from.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "PolicyGradient": {"video": "videos/PolicyGradient.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Step three, the policy step. Three real steps from the batch: the readout's odds, the action it drew, and the advantage: better than expected, about as expected, worse.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.7333, "end": 5.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The rule is one line: raise the probability of the action taken when the advantage is positive, lower it when negative. These are the real changes after this batch's twenty epochs.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "ClipRatio": {"video": "videos/ClipRatio.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Step four, the clip. Per step, one number: the new probability of its action over the old one. For a good step the plain rule says the bigger the better, without limit; PPO bends that line flat at one point two, so past twenty percent more likely the step stops pushing. A bad step is the mirror image, down to zero point eight. Going the wrong way is never capped.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.7333, "end": 7.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The real batch under that rule. Each dot is a step: ratio across, advantage up. Over twenty epochs good steps drift right, bad ones left, and those past the walls stop being pushed.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Epochs": {"video": "videos/Epochs.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Step five. The same batch is used twenty times: twenty epochs, each one moving W a little. The ring is not used again: the epochs are arithmetic on the stored spectra.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.7333, "end": 8.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Then the batch is discarded: it was played by the old policy. Sixty-four games play again with the new W, using the ring once per step, and the cycle repeats: sixty times for CartPole.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "RankOne": {"video": "videos/RankOne.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.2833, "kind": "normal", "note": "Now the part that matters most: what exactly happens to W. Here is W at this iteration, two rows of seventeen, and the batch. Take one recorded step out of it.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.2833, "end": 4.3, "kind": "normal", "note": "The step holds the spectrum phi: lay it along the top, one number per column. It holds the action drawn, and we know the odds. Action minus odds: what I did, minus what I expected to do. One number per row.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.3, "end": 7.6833, "kind": "normal", "note": "Multiply every row number by every column number: a grid the shape of W, this step's push on every weight. One column times one row: a rank-one picture. Red pushes a weight up, blue down.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.6833, "end": 10.0167, "kind": "normal", "note": "Scale it by the step's advantage. Better than expected: the push counts with a plus sign. Worse would flip every colour: do less of that.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 10.0167, "end": 13.8827, "kind": "normal", "note": "Every recorded step makes one of these. Add all 2048.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 13.8827, "end": 17.9324, "kind": "normal", "note": "That average is the direction. W takes a small step along it: the new W is the old W plus a little of this grid; Adam sizes the step for each weight. The two biases move the same way, with a one in place of the spectrum. Then the next epoch: the same 2048 stored steps, read again; nothing is played and the ring is not run. Only the odds are worked out again with the new W, so the push changes a little each time. Twenty epochs per batch.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 17.9324, "end": 18.9824, "kind": "normal", "note": "Look at what the optics contributed: only phi, the row along the top, and it was stored with the batch. Nothing in this update touches the ring.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WLearns": {"video": "videos/WLearns.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 10.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The whole training run, the real one, in one sweep: 60 iterations of play a batch, twenty epochs. W starts as all zeros, so at first the agent pushes at random and the pole drops. The grid is the real W after each iteration, the curve is the return, and the cart plays with the W it has at that moment, twice real time. By the end these thirty-four weights balance the pole for the full five hundred steps. Nothing else in the system changed.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "AskOnce": {"video": "videos/AskOnce.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Where the ring sits in one training iteration. First the games are played: 64 games times 32 steps, and every step goes through the ring once, so the ring is used 2048 times. Each step's spectrum phi is stored, with the action taken and, once the games are scored, its advantage. That is the batch.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.7333, "end": 8.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Then the 20 epochs. Each one rereads those same 2048 stored steps: their phi, their action, their advantage. It computes the odds with the current W and takes one gradient step on W. No game is played again and the ring is not run again: phi is already stored. So yes, the ring shapes everything W learns, because every phi in the batch came out of it. What it never does is change, or carry a gradient: the gradient goes back to W and stops there. That is why a real ring can sit in this loop. Over the 60 iterations that is about 123 thousand ring readings.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Sec5": {"video": "videos/Sec5.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Part five: why bother with the ring at all. The readout is only a linear map, so what it can do depends on what the comb hands it.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.1333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "XorCut": {"video": "videos/XorCut.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.2833, "kind": "normal", "note": "Start with XOR: two inputs, red where they agree, blue where they differ. A linear readout draws one straight line, and at least one point is always on the wrong side.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.2833, "end": 6.5833, "kind": "normal", "note": "Hand the readout one more number, the product of the two inputs, on the vertical axis. Now one straight cut splits them.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 6.5833, "end": 10.8833, "kind": "normal", "note": "The same cut drawn back in the two inputs bends into a hyperbola. A straight cut in the new feature is a bent cut in the inputs.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WhyProducts": {"video": "videos/WhyProducts.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 6.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Monday's question: why is a product more nonlinear than a linear layer? First, how to read these maps. Each point is one state of the pendulum: its angle across, from hanging through upright to hanging, and how fast it spins, up the side. Take a state, give it to the readout, see which way it pushes, and colour the point: red counter-clockwise, blue clockwise. Do that for every point and you get the map; the white line is where the choice flips. This is Pasha's trained linear readout: weights on the three inputs only.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 6.3333, "end": 8.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Right: the same kind of readout, but it also gets the six products of pairs of inputs, like cos theta times theta dot. On the left, the flip line is wherever a weighted sum of the three inputs is zero: a flat cut in those three numbers, which drawn over angle and spin is this one wave, and no choice of weights does more. Stacking a second linear layer doesn't help: W two times W one is one matrix again. With the products, the same flat cut in nine numbers comes out bent and closed. And pair products are exactly what the ring makes, from part three.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "PolicyMaps": {"video": "videos/PolicyMaps.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 6.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Now the ring. The same kind of map: every state goes through the encoding, the ring in Pasha's solver and the trained readout, and gets the colour of the torque picked. Left the linear readout, right the ring; both are Pasha's published readouts. The white line is one swing-up attempt from hanging. The linear readout has a single border, so its pendulum pushes one way and circles near the bottom. The ring's map flips sign with theta dot and with cosine theta, and its pendulum goes over the top and is caught. Average return: minus 718 against minus 252.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 6.2333, "end": 9.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Which products does the ring's map use? I fit the ring's torque map with a weighted sum of simple terms and ask what share of the map the fit reproduces: that is R squared, one meaning all of it. With the three inputs alone it is 0.30. Add their six pair products and it is 0.84. The largest term by far is theta dot times cosine theta: a pair product, the kind the ring makes, and the readout uses it. For CartPole and the lander a linear readout does as well as the ring; those slides are in the appendix.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Sec6": {"video": "videos/Sec6.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Last part: what comes next. A direct test of the ring's nonlinearity, where this scheme fits and where it doesn't yet, what a lattice of rings might buy, and how far the optics could reach.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.1333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "XorTest": {"video": "videos/XorTest.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.2833, "kind": "normal", "note": "First direction: Mohammad's XOR test of the nonlinearity. Two tones, each low or high; call them zero and one, so four inputs, through the same ring: four spectra. For a linear ring this is boring: combine the four outputs the XOR way and you get zero. Whatever is left is the nonlinearity.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.2833, "end": 4.1833, "kind": "normal", "note": "How to score it? Monday's answer: four classes. Measure every line up to some highest line, run a PCA, and see how far apart the four classes sit; if they are well apart, it is a good XOR. Here it is on the talk's ring, with Pasha's one percent detector noise, 200 readings per input: four tight clouds. Mohammad's own caveat: this needs every line measured, or a multiplexer, so it is not a lab measurement yet. He also wants to see it in the other regimes.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "SmallInputs": {"video": "videos/SmallInputs.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Where does this fit? Every input gets its own tone. CartPole: four tones beside the pump, seventeen lines read, ten pair products.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 5.1333, "end": 10.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "LunarLander: eight tones, thirty-three lines, thirty-six pair products. Still comfortable.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 10.3333, "end": 17.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Bigger games are another story, and these are reinforcement-learning games too. Connect four: forty-two cells, so forty-two tones, already past what a real ring has room for: Pasha's estimate is eighty to a hundred usable lines, about twenty inputs with this encoding. Chess, one input per square and piece type: 768. Atari, as DQN sees it: four frames of 84 by 84 pixels, 28,224. One tone each overflows the comb. The boards and frames are schematics. So today this is for control tasks with a handful of inputs. What we are trying next is to make it work as a general neural network. That is hard: to keep it all optical, the training has to get around backprop, because the gradient cannot be sent back through the light.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "PhaseSpaceMap": {"video": "videos/PhaseSpaceMap.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Next direction: grow the reservoir. One ring becomes a lattice of coupled rings, like Lida's. For one ring we have the map from part two. For a lattice there is no general model of its phase space yet: nobody has that map. Mohammad's suggestion is to scan it and let a classifier sort the states by itself, unsupervised.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.3333, "end": 4.2333, "kind": "normal", "note": "And we don't know, in general, whether more rings buy us anything. The candidates from my board: richer states in phase space; an easier experiment; topological protection, if that is even useful here; more control over epsilon; a denser feature space; memory for an optical critic. The one I have tried is the last: Lida's lattice as the critic's reservoir. On Pendulum it beat a linear critic, but mostly by remembering the last step, not through the Kerr effect; on LunarLander nothing separated. So it is open.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "AllOptical": {"video": "videos/AllOptical.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "This much is optics today: the tones, the ring and its comb. Everything else, squashing the numbers, the readout, the softmax, the training, is a computer.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 1.7333, "end": 2.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Can we push that boundary? One candidate is this guy, the squash in front of the tones.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.5333, "end": 3.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Or this guy: the readout, a weighted sum of line powers, which is the only trained part.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.3333, "end": 4.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "And, from Mohammad, two more. The softmax: his idea with Pasha is two more rings. And the training itself: backprop cannot run through the light, so it would take a rule that needs no gradients passed back, like equilibrium propagation. His view is that it is too early. But that is the road to something fully optical that the lab could build.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Thanks": {"video": "videos/Thanks.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Thank you. All of this stands on Pasha's code and on what Lida, Shi-Yuan, Mahmoud and Mohammad taught me these past weeks. Happy to take questions.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.1333, "end": 10.4333, "kind": "loop", "note": "", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "Appendix": {"video": "videos/Appendix.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 1.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Backup slides, for questions.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppCartPole": {"video": "videos/AppCartPole.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "For reference: CartPole's rules. One point per step until the pole passes twelve degrees or the cart leaves the track, capped at five hundred steps.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppPendulum": {"video": "videos/AppPendulum.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pendulum's rules. Every step costs the squared angle from upright plus a little for speed and torque, for two hundred steps; nothing ends the game early.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppLander": {"video": "videos/AppLander.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The lander's rules. Each step is scored by how much closer, slower and more upright it got, minus fuel; plus or minus a hundred at the end. Two hundred counts as landed.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppPasha": {"video": "videos/AppPasha.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pasha's settings, for the record. Left, PPO: sixty-four games, thirty-two steps, twenty passes, clip at 0.2. Right, the ring in the pattern-free regime that every run in this talk uses.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppExplored": {"video": "videos/AppExplored.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "What I played with in my own repositories. Everything here was re-run on my template and reproduces, but I have not re-derived it, so treat it as preliminary. Several of October's conclusions flipped in the re-run.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "PendulumNeeds": {"video": "videos/PendulumNeeds.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.55, "kind": "normal", "note": "The game that needs it: the trained ring, starting from hanging. At the bottom the torque points along the motion, so each swing gains energy. On the right, the same motion in the plane of angle and speed.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 5.55, "end": 12.05, "kind": "normal", "note": "One more push and it has enough energy. Near the top the torque turns against the motion: it brakes, to arrive with no speed left.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 12.05, "end": 16.15, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pump below the horizontal, brake above it: the sign of minus theta dot times cosine theta. Over the plane that is a checkerboard, and the real run follows it. A product of two inputs, like XOR.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 16.15, "end": 23.75, "kind": "normal", "note": "And this is the best a linear readout found: one wavy border. It cannot follow a checkerboard, so from hanging it pushes one way for ever and never reaches the top.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "LineFour": {"video": "videos/LineFour.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 6.5333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Keep that red bar and ask where the ring makes it. Four-wave mixing takes a photon each from lines one and three and puts them on zero and four: cosine theta times theta dot. Two photons from line two can land there too.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 6.5333, "end": 9.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Is that the line the readout uses? Split the red bar by comb line: line four carries 40 percent of it, and its mirror, minus four, another 22.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 9.7333, "end": 20.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Two processes land on line four, so which carries the product? Line four follows theta dot cosine theta with partial correlation 0.83. Freeze the sine tone: 0.88. Switch it off: still 0.88. It comes in through one plus three.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 20.3333, "end": 26.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "In one line: line four's light is the pump times a-one a-three plus a-two squared, plus smaller terms. A-one a-three contains theta dot cosine theta; a-two squared only knows sine theta.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "RacePendulum": {"video": "videos/RacePendulum.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pendulum: linear readout left, ring right, same start, same random draws. At update zero both push at random. Every replay is real, at double speed.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.7333, "end": 16.8833, "kind": "normal", "note": "Training runs: the pens draw both returns, and at each stop both replay that start. By update 35 the ring pumps, goes over and holds. The linear readout only swings.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 16.8833, "end": 23.6667, "kind": "normal", "note": "After 250 updates the ring is up in three seconds; the linear readout can pump or brake, not both. On 64 fresh starts: linear −745, ring −245. Dashed: explicit pair products, −160.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "LanderTruth": {"video": "videos/LanderTruth.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 9.3, "kind": "normal", "note": "The lander, and Shi-Yuan's question: does it need a nonlinearity at all? At update zero both crash. At update 25 the ring lands slowly and the linear one touches down but keeps firing.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 9.3, "end": 19.0167, "kind": "normal", "note": "By update 75 both land between the flags, and both curves end above 200, the score that counts as landed.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 19.0167, "end": 24.8667, "kind": "normal", "note": "One run proves nothing, so here is every run, Pasha's code unchanged. Linear clears 200 in 30 of 40 runs, explicit products in 34 of 40, and the ring, in the regime we use, in all 4 that exist.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 24.8667, "end": 27.4667, "kind": "normal", "note": "The hollow dots are the three published linear runs: 106, 25, 7. Real, but a seed doesn't reproduce across machines, and linear fails one time in four. So Shi-Yuan was right: LunarLander doesn't need the nonlinearity. Pendulum does.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "CartPoleHonest": {"video": "videos/CartPoleHonest.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.9, "kind": "normal", "note": "CartPole, the honest counterexample. At update zero both fall in a quarter of a second. A fallen pole is only twelve degrees over: that is where the game stops.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 2.9, "end": 11.35, "kind": "normal", "note": "A few updates in, at double speed, both last longer. By update 10 neither drops the pole; they drift off the end of the track instead.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 11.35, "end": 20.1, "kind": "normal", "note": "By update 20 both balance for the full 500 steps. Linear scores 500, the ring 500. The ring buys nothing here.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 20.1, "end": 26.25, "kind": "normal", "note": "The scorecard. CartPole and LunarLander need only a straight cut: linear is enough. Pendulum needs a product, theta-dot times cos theta: that is where the ring earns its place.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WhyOffset": {"video": "videos/WhyOffset.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 8.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Why the plus one? Without it the amplitude follows the number, sign and all. Tilt the pole left, then right: the amplitude flips sign, but a detector reads power, so both poses look the same.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 8.1333, "end": 15.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "With the offset the amplitude never changes sign, so the power climbs all the way: pole left is dark, pole right is bright. These curves are a schematic of the tone's own power.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 15.0333, "end": 17.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "It shows in training. Pasha ran CartPole both ways: about 102 steps with the signed encoding, the full 500 with the offset. One seed, chaotic ring.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "ClosedBox": {"video": "videos/ClosedBox.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "This is all we use about the reservoir. Five lines of light go in: the pump and one tone per number. A comb comes out, and we read seventeen lines.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 5.3333, "end": 11.6333, "kind": "loop", "note": "Live, in a real game: the tones follow the cart and the pole, and the comb answers at every step.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 11.6333, "end": 15.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Inside is a Kerr microring next to a waveguide; the drawing is a schematic. How it works is Lida's talk. Nothing in there is trained.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "BeyondReadout": {"video": "videos/BeyondReadout.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Two more directions. Today only the readout learns, in red. The critic and the softmax are still electronics: both are candidates to move into the optics.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 5.7333, "end": 9.7333, "kind": "normal", "note": "To train anything before the readout, the gradient would have to pass back through the ring, and it cannot: we only store what the ring answered. Training without backpropagation exists; Mohammad's view: too early.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 9.7333, "end": 16.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "And the reservoir can grow: one ring becomes a lattice of coupled rings, Lida's lattices. Each comb line splits into a cluster of fine lines: a comb made of combs. This picture is a schematic.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "WhyPPO": {"video": "videos/WhyPPO.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 4.0333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Why PPO. The oldest recipe, the plain policy gradient: play a batch of games, take one small step towards the actions that paid off, discard the batch. On our own system, five runs: it gets there, slowly.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 4.0333, "end": 7.3333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The obvious fix: twenty passes over each batch, not one. It reaches the top sooner, but the batch was played by the old policy, and the policy has moved away from it: these runs get to the top and fall apart.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 7.3333, "end": 10.6333, "kind": "normal", "note": "PPO is the same reuse with a clip: no action's probability is rewarded for changing by more than twenty percent per batch. Same twenty passes; now it gets there and stays.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 10.6333, "end": 13.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Gradients only, few knobs, the same settings across very different problems: that is why it became the default. OpenAI's Dota bots used it, and the feedback stage of InstructGPT. Here it trains thirty-six numbers.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "LogitLooks": {"video": "videos/LogitLooks.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 3.1333, "kind": "normal", "note": "I also played with how the readout uses the lines the ring makes. This is the trained CartPole readout: the weight for push right minus the weight for push left, line by line. Blue lines are the ones we drive, lavender the ones the ring made. About two thirds of the weight sits on the new lines.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}, {"start": 3.1333, "end": 7.4333, "kind": "normal", "note": "And because the ring settles, a logit is just a function of the state. Here it is over the pole's angle and how fast it turns, cart at rest: red, push right; blue, push left; the white line is where they tie. For CartPole it is close to a tilted plane. The thin track is a real game: it stays near the middle, and there most of the swing still comes from the lines we drive.", "auto": false, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppPipeline": {"video": "videos/AppPipeline.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "For reference, the same loop as a block diagram, from my October talk. One correction to the drawing: the squash is a tanh for CartPole and LunarLander and a plain clip for Pendulum.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppRegimes": {"video": "videos/AppRegimes.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Pasha's figure of the four states the ring can sit in. Left: the light around the ring over time. Middle: its final profile. Right: the comb. Every run in this talk uses the second row: normal dispersion, no patterns, one stable state for each input and no noise of its own.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppExpressivity": {"video": "videos/AppExpressivity.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary, from my October talk. Top row: how nonlinear the ring's response is as the tones get stronger. Bottom row: how well a linear readout recovers the inputs, their pair products and their triple products. Each regime has a window, and more nonlinearity is not more computing. This was drafted with AI help and I have not re-derived it yet.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppCollisions": {"video": "videos/AppCollisions.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary, also from October. With more inputs the pair products get harder to read out. Consecutive tones collide: lines one plus four and two plus three feed the same new line. Placing the tones so that no two pairs collide helps until detector noise wins, and it costs more lines. Drafted with AI help, not re-derived yet.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppLines": {"video": "videos/AppLines.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary, from my ring-requirements study. How many lines does the readout need? For Pendulum, five to nine lines picked by a lasso do as well as all seventeen. LunarLander lands from about thirteen. A few seeds only, and not re-derived yet.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppPhase": {"video": "videos/AppPhase.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary. What if the phases of the input tones drift? The original ring, black, tolerates a tenth of a radian and fails when the tones are unlocked. With collision-free tones and training without a phase lock, red, it does not care. Three seeds, not re-derived yet.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppPower": {"video": "videos/AppPower.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary. Turn the pump down and see when the policy breaks. With one percent relative detector noise it barely matters. With shot and receiver noise it depends on how long the detector integrates: a microsecond holds much further down than a third of a nanosecond. Turning this into watts assumes a one square micron mode area and an estimated coupling g zero, so I do not quote watts. Not re-derived yet.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppCritic": {"video": "videos/AppCritic.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary, from my optical-critic study. Can the critic read the comb as well? A critic on the actor's lines clearly beats having no critic, but the MLP critic still wins. I make no claim from this yet.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "AppLattice": {"video": "videos/AppLattice.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "Preliminary. The same question with Lida's lattice as the critic's reservoir. Lower is better here. On Pendulum it beats a plain linear critic, but most of that comes from remembering the previous step, not from the Kerr effect. On LunarLander nothing separates.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}, "References": {"video": "videos/References.mp4", "clicks": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 0.8333, "kind": "normal", "note": "The full references, for the record.", "auto": true, "after": 0.0}]}};
